  §34.5-§34.7 printed in full (short sections; the pointer at L9580 is tested on the text):
    L9609 ### 34.5 The corridor
    L9611 Requiring the observed subshell to have least ν gives one linear inequality per
    L9612 rival, and their intersection is an interval:
    L9614 > **L(Z) < a < U(Z)**,  **L, U = Δn(√p_g + √p_r)/(p_g − p_r)**
    L9616 **All 106 non-empty. Nineteen distinct surds across the whole table. No
    L9617 measurement enters — only Pauli's capacity and the node count.**
    L9619 *For every ns/(n−1)d competition the crossing is (√(n−1) + √(n−4))/3, giving
    L9620 0.5773503, 1.0000000, 1.2168450, 1.3938270 at n = 4 to 7.*
    L9622 ### 34.6 The walk
    L9624 **`a` is carried between elements and resets eighteen times.** Every reset is a
    L9625 **subshell opening** (8), an **aufbau exception** (6), or the **return from one**
    L9626 (4) — Tc after Mo, Tb after Gd, Bk after Cm, Hg after Au. **It never resets
    L9627 mid-subshell**, which is why each subshell fills at constant `a`.
    L9629 **And the state is necessary.** With no carried value, 104 of 106 steps admit two
    L9630 to four self-consistent subshells. *The observed one is always among them and
    L9631 never uniquely determined. The periodic table is not computable from a single
    L9632 atom's configuration.*
    L9634 ### 34.7 The entry point
    L9636 Where `a` sits in its corridor is fixed by the single-state observation — the
    L9637 ionisation energy — and the position is
    L9639 > **t(ℓ) → √( ℓ(ℓ+1) / 2 )**
    L9641 **Measured 1.028 at p across four subshells and 1.785 at d across two: the ratio
    L9642 is √3 to 0.19%.** *ℓ(ℓ+1) is the centrifugal term, the only place ℓ enters the
    L9643 radial Schrödinger equation.*
    L9645 **And it is a limit approached along n**, not a constant: the p row of Λ_t reads
    L9646 1.120, 1.049, 1.022, 1.002 at n = 3, 4, 5, 6 — **6p sits 0.2% above the
    L9647 centrifugal value.**

  the two out-of-unit sites of 108 (from test 5), read:
